from tn import *
def C(ch,*a,**k): TN(9,"maths",ch,*a,**k)
C("set-language","Asha sorted her class into the group of students who like cricket and the group who like football. \"Some are in both groups,\" she noticed.",
 ["A set is a well-defined collection of objects. A set can be written in descriptive form, in set-builder form such as {x : x is a vowel} or in roster form such as {a, e, i, o, u}. Sets are named by capital letters and their members are called elements, which belong to the set.",
  "There are many types of sets. The empty set has no element, a finite set has a countable number of elements and an infinite set does not end. Equal sets have the same elements, equivalent sets have the same number of elements, a subset is contained in another set and the power set of A is the set of all subsets of A, with 2^n elements if A has n elements.",
  "Set operations are the union (all elements of A or B), intersection (elements common to both), difference and complement. De Morgan's laws say (A union B)' = A' intersect B' and (A intersect B)' = A' union B'. For two finite sets, n(A union B) = n(A) + n(B) - n(A intersect B)."],
 ["set","subset","power set","union","intersection","De Morgan's laws"],
 ("If n(A) = 10, n(B) = 8 and n(A intersect B) = 3, find n(A union B).",["Use n(A union B) = n(A) + n(B) - n(A intersect B).","10 + 8 - 3 = 15.","So n(A union B) = 15."]),
 ("The empty set has one element.",["The empty set has no elements.","Its cardinality is 0."]),
 ["A set is a well-defined collection.","Sets have roster, set-builder and descriptive forms.","A set with n elements has 2^n subsets.","n(A union B) = n(A) + n(B) - n(A intersect B)."],
 ["m|The roster form of vowels is|{a, e, i, o, u}|{a, b, c}|{x : x > 0}|It lists the elements.","t|The empty set has no elements.|T|Yes.","m|A set with 3 elements has how many subsets?|8|6|9|2^3 = 8.","t|The union contains only common elements.|F|That is the intersection.","m|The elements common to A and B form the|intersection|union|complement|A intersect B.","s|How many subsets does a 2-element set have?|4;four|2^2 = 4.","m|(A union B)' equals|A' intersect B'|A' union B'|A intersect B|De Morgan's law.","t|Equal sets have the same elements.|T|Yes."])
C("real-numbers","Meena asked her teacher, \"Is every number a fraction?\" The teacher smiled and wrote the square root of 2.",
 ["Rational numbers can be written as p/q with q not zero, and their decimals either end or repeat. Irrational numbers cannot be written like this, and their decimals neither end nor repeat, as with the square root of 2 and pi. Together rational and irrational numbers form the real numbers, which fill the number line.",
  "The radical notation n-th root of a is written with a radical sign. A surd is an irrational root such as the square root of 3. Surds have properties, and we can add and subtract like surds and multiply any surds. Rationalisation removes the surd from the denominator by multiplying the top and bottom by a suitable surd.",
  "Scientific notation writes a number as a x 10^n with 1 <= a < 10, which helps us write very large and very small numbers such as 5.9 x 10^24."],
 ["rational number","irrational number","real number","surd","rationalisation","scientific notation"],
 ("Rationalise 1 / sqrt(2).",["Multiply top and bottom by sqrt(2).","The denominator becomes 2.","So 1 / sqrt(2) = sqrt(2) / 2."]),
 ("Every real number is rational.",["Numbers like the square root of 2 are irrational.","Real numbers include both."]),
 ["Rational: p/q form.","Irrational decimals never end or repeat.","Rationalisation clears a surd in the denominator.","Scientific notation uses a x 10^n."],
 ["m|Which is irrational?|sqrt(2)|0.5|3/4|It cannot be written as p/q.","t|The decimal of a rational number ends or repeats.|T|Yes.","m|5,000 in scientific notation is|5 x 10^3|50 x 10^2|0.5 x 10^4|1 <= a < 10.","t|Pi is a rational number.|F|It is irrational.","m|Rationalising 1 / sqrt(3) gives|sqrt(3) / 3|3 / sqrt(3)|1 / 3|Multiply by sqrt(3).","s|Is 0.333... rational? Answer yes or no.|yes|It equals 1/3.","m|The real numbers are|rational and irrational numbers together|only integers|only fractions|They fill the number line.","t|A surd is an irrational root.|T|Yes."])
C("algebra","Raju saw the expression 3x + 5 on the board. \"What is its value when x is 2?\" asked his teacher.",
 ["A polynomial is an expression with whole-number powers of a variable, named by degree as linear, quadratic or cubic, and by terms as monomial, binomial or trinomial. The value of a polynomial is found by putting a number for the variable, and a zero of the polynomial is a value that makes it 0.",
  "The remainder theorem says that when p(x) is divided by (x - a), the remainder is p(a). The factor theorem says that (x - a) is a factor of p(x) if and only if p(a) = 0. Algebraic identities such as (a + b)^2 = a^2 + 2ab + b^2, (a - b)^2 and a^2 - b^2 = (a + b)(a - b) help us expand and factorise.",
  "Factorisation writes a polynomial as a product, by identities or by splitting the middle term of a quadratic trinomial. Polynomials are divided by the division algorithm or by synthetic division. The GCD of polynomials is the highest common factor, and a linear equation in two variables has a pair of values that satisfy it."],
 ["polynomial","zero of a polynomial","remainder theorem","algebraic identity","factorisation","GCD"],
 ("Find the remainder when x^2 + 2x + 1 is divided by (x - 1).",["By the remainder theorem put x = 1.","1 + 2 + 1 = 4.","The remainder is 4."]),
 ("(a + b)^2 = a^2 + b^2.",["The middle term 2ab is also present.","(a + b)^2 = a^2 + 2ab + b^2."]),
 ["Polynomials have whole-number powers.","Remainder = p(a).","a^2 - b^2 = (a + b)(a - b).","Factor theorem uses p(a) = 0."],
 ["m|The degree of x^2 + 3x + 2 is|2|3|1|The highest power.","t|(a - b)^2 = a^2 - 2ab + b^2.|T|Yes.","m|The remainder when p(x) is divided by (x - 2) is|p(2)|p(-2)|p(0)|Remainder theorem.","t|x^2 - 9 = (x + 3)(x - 3).|T|Difference of squares.","m|A polynomial with two terms is a|binomial|trinomial|monomial|Like x + 1.","s|What is the value of 3x + 5 at x = 2?|11|3(2) + 5 = 11.","m|If p(a) = 0, then (x - a) is a|factor|constant|remainder|Factor theorem.","t|Quadratic polynomials have degree 2.|T|Yes."])
C("geometry","Asha folded a paper to make a triangle and then a rectangle. \"Shapes follow rules,\" said her teacher.",
 ["Two lines cut by a transversal make pairs of angles: corresponding, alternate and co-interior. The angles of a triangle add up to 180 degrees and its exterior angle equals the sum of the two opposite interior angles. Congruent triangles are of the same shape and size and are proved by the SSS, SAS, ASA and RHS rules.",
  "A quadrilateral has four sides, and its angles add up to 360 degrees. Special types are the parallelogram, rectangle, rhombus, square, trapezium and kite. In a parallelogram, opposite sides and opposite angles are equal and the diagonals bisect each other.",
  "A circle has parts such as the centre, radius, chord, diameter, arc and segment. The perpendicular from the centre to a chord bisects the chord, equal chords subtend equal angles at the centre, and a cyclic quadrilateral has opposite angles that add up to 180 degrees. Practical geometry constructs these figures with a ruler and compass."],
 ["transversal","congruent triangles","parallelogram","chord","cyclic quadrilateral","construction"],
 ("Two angles of a triangle are 50 and 60 degrees. Find the third.",["The sum of angles is 180.","50 + 60 = 110.","180 - 110 = 70 degrees."]),
 ("The angles of a quadrilateral add up to 180 degrees.",["They add up to 360 degrees.","A triangle's angles add up to 180."]),
 ["Triangle angles sum to 180 degrees.","Quadrilateral angles sum to 360 degrees.","Perpendicular from the centre bisects a chord.","Cyclic quadrilateral opposite angles sum to 180."],
 ["m|The angle sum of a triangle is|180 degrees|360 degrees|90 degrees|Always.","t|Diagonals of a parallelogram bisect each other.|T|Yes.","m|The angle sum of a quadrilateral is|360 degrees|180 degrees|270 degrees|Two triangles.","t|SSS is a congruence rule.|T|Yes.","m|Opposite angles of a cyclic quadrilateral add up to|180 degrees|90 degrees|360 degrees|A property of a circle.","s|What is the third angle if two are 50 and 60 degrees?|70|180 - 110 = 70.","m|A chord through the centre is a|diameter|radius|arc|Longest chord.","t|A rhombus has four equal sides.|T|Yes."])
C("coordinate-geometry","Meena found her seat in the cinema hall by row and seat number. \"Two numbers locate a place,\" said her teacher.",
 ["The Cartesian plane is made by two number lines, the x-axis and the y-axis, which meet at the origin. A point is located by an ordered pair (x, y), where x is the abscissa and y the ordinate. The axes divide the plane into four quadrants.",
  "In the first quadrant both coordinates are positive, in the second x is negative and y positive, in the third both are negative and in the fourth x is positive and y negative. Points on the x-axis have y = 0 and points on the y-axis have x = 0.",
  "The distance between two points (x1, y1) and (x2, y2) is the square root of (x2 - x1)^2 + (y2 - y1)^2, and the section formula finds the point dividing the segment in a given ratio."],
 ["Cartesian plane","origin","ordered pair","quadrant","distance formula","section formula"],
 ("Find the distance between (0, 0) and (3, 4).",["Use the distance formula.","sqrt(3^2 + 4^2) = sqrt(25).","The distance is 5 units."]),
 ("The point (-2, 3) lies in the first quadrant.",["Its x is negative and y is positive.","So it lies in the second quadrant."]),
 ["Two axes meet at the origin.","(x, y) is an ordered pair.","Four quadrants.","Distance = sqrt((x2 - x1)^2 + (y2 - y1)^2)."],
 ["m|The origin is|(0, 0)|(1, 1)|(0, 1)|Where axes meet.","t|A point on the x-axis has y = 0.|T|Yes.","m|The point (-3, -4) is in the|third quadrant|first quadrant|fourth quadrant|Both negative.","t|(2, 3) and (3, 2) are the same point.|F|The order matters.","m|The distance of (3, 4) from the origin is|5|7|1|sqrt(9 + 16).","s|In (4, 7), which number is the abscissa?|4|The x-coordinate.","m|The y-axis is the|vertical axis|horizontal axis|diagonal|Up and down.","t|The plane has four quadrants.|T|Yes."])
C("trigonometry","Raju looked at a ladder leaning on a wall. \"How can we find its angle?\" he wondered.",
 ["In a right-angled triangle, the sides are the hypotenuse, the opposite side and the adjacent side with respect to an angle. The trigonometric ratios are sine = opposite / hypotenuse, cosine = adjacent / hypotenuse and tangent = opposite / adjacent, and their reciprocals are cosecant, secant and cotangent.",
  "For complementary angles, sin(90 - A) = cos A, cos(90 - A) = sin A and tan(90 - A) = cot A. Values of the ratios for 0, 30, 45, 60 and 90 degrees are found in the trigonometric table, such as sin 30 = 1/2, cos 60 = 1/2 and tan 45 = 1.",
  "The method of using the trigonometric table lets us find the ratio of any angle, and these ideas help to find heights and distances."],
 ["hypotenuse","sine, cosine, tangent","reciprocal ratios","complementary angles","trigonometric table","height and distance"],
 ("In a right triangle opposite = 3 and hypotenuse = 5. Find sin A.",["sin A = opposite / hypotenuse.","3 / 5.","sin A = 3/5."]),
 ("sin 30 = 1.",["sin 30 = 1/2.","sin 90 is 1."]),
 ["sin = opp / hyp.","cos = adj / hyp.","tan = opp / adj.","sin(90 - A) = cos A."],
 ["m|sin A equals|opposite / hypotenuse|adjacent / hypotenuse|opposite / adjacent|Definition.","t|tan 45 = 1.|T|Yes.","m|cos(90 - A) equals|sin A|cos A|tan A|Complementary angles.","t|The hypotenuse is the shortest side.|F|It is the longest.","m|sin 30 equals|1/2|1|sqrt(3)/2|From the table.","s|What is tan 45?|1|Equal opposite and adjacent.","m|Reciprocal of sine is|cosecant|secant|cotangent|1 / sin A.","t|Trigonometry helps find heights.|T|Yes."])
C("mensuration","Asha wanted to paint a box-shaped cupboard. \"How much paint?\" she asked. \"Find the surface area,\" said her teacher.",
 ["Heron's formula gives the area of a triangle from its three sides a, b and c: area = sqrt(s(s - a)(s - b)(s - c)) where s is half the perimeter. It is used to find the area of a triangle when no height is known, and to find the area of a quadrilateral by splitting it into two triangles.",
  "A cuboid has length l, breadth b and height h. Its total surface area is 2(lb + bh + hl), its lateral surface area is 2h(l + b) and its volume is l x b x h. A cube of side a has surface area 6a^2 and volume a^3.",
  "Area is measured in square units and volume in cubic units."],
 ["Heron's formula","semi-perimeter","cuboid","lateral surface area","cube","volume"],
 ("Find the volume of a cube of side 4 cm.",["Volume = a^3.","4 x 4 x 4 = 64.","The volume is 64 cubic cm."]),
 ("Volume of a cuboid is l + b + h.",["Volume is the product l x b x h.","Adding does not give volume."]),
 ["Heron's formula uses s.","Cuboid volume = lbh.","Cube surface area = 6a^2.","Volume is in cubic units."],
 ["m|The volume of a cuboid is|l x b x h|2(lb + bh + hl)|l + b + h|Product of dimensions.","t|A cube has six equal faces.|T|Yes.","m|The surface area of a cube of side a is|6a^2|a^3|4a^2|Six faces.","t|Heron's formula needs the height.|F|It uses the three sides.","m|The semi-perimeter s of sides 3, 4, 5 is|6|12|3|(3 + 4 + 5) / 2.","s|Volume of a cube of side 4 cm in cubic cm?|64|4^3.","m|Volume is measured in|cubic units|square units|metres only|Like cm^3.","t|Lateral area leaves out top and bottom.|T|Yes."])
C("statistics","Meena asked each classmate their height and wrote the numbers down. \"What is the typical height?\" she wondered.",
 ["Statistics deals with collecting, organising and understanding data. Data collected first-hand is primary data and data taken from published sources is secondary data. Raw data is arranged in frequency tables to see patterns.",
  "A measure of central tendency gives one value that represents the data. The arithmetic mean is the sum of all values divided by the number of values. The median is the middle value when the data is arranged in order, and the mode is the value that occurs most often.",
  "For an even number of values, the median is the average of the two middle values. These measures help us compare data sets."],
 ["data","frequency","mean","median","mode","central tendency"],
 ("Find the mean of 2, 4, 6, 8.",["Add the values: 2 + 4 + 6 + 8 = 20.","There are 4 values.","Mean = 20 / 4 = 5."]),
 ("The mode is the middle value.",["The median is the middle value.","The mode occurs most often."]),
 ["Mean = sum / count.","Median is the middle value.","Mode is the most frequent value.","Data can be primary or secondary."],
 ["m|The mean of 2, 4, 6 is|4|6|12|12 / 3.","t|The mode is the most frequent value.|T|Yes.","m|The median of 1, 3, 5 is|3|1|5|The middle.","t|Mean is the middle value.|F|That is the median.","m|Data collected first-hand is|primary data|secondary data|raw data only|You collect it yourself.","s|What is the mode of 2, 3, 3, 4?|3|It appears twice.","m|The median of an even number of values is the|average of the middle two|largest value|smallest value|Add and halve.","t|Mean, median and mode are measures of central tendency.|T|Yes."])
C("probability","Raju tossed a coin and called heads. \"What are the chances?\" asked his teacher.",
 ["An experiment is an action with possible outcomes, like tossing a coin, and the set of all outcomes is the sample space. An event is a part of the sample space. In the classical approach, probability P(E) = number of favourable outcomes / total number of outcomes, so it lies between 0 and 1.",
  "The empirical approach finds probability from experiments: P(E) = number of times the event happened / total number of trials. The more trials, the closer it comes to the classical value.",
  "Types of events include the sure event with probability 1, the impossible event with probability 0 and equally likely, mutually exclusive and complementary events, where P(E) + P(not E) = 1."],
 ["sample space","event","classical probability","empirical probability","sure event","complementary event"],
 ("A die is thrown. Find the probability of getting an even number.",["There are 6 equally likely outcomes.","Even numbers are 2, 4 and 6, that is 3.","P = 3/6 = 1/2."]),
 ("Probability can be 2.",["Probability lies from 0 to 1.","A value of 2 is impossible."]),
 ["P(E) lies between 0 and 1.","P(E) = favourable / total.","Sure event: 1; impossible event: 0.","P(E) + P(not E) = 1."],
 ["m|The probability of heads on a fair coin is|1/2|1|0|Two equally likely outcomes.","t|Probability of a sure event is 1.|T|Yes.","m|The probability of an impossible event is|0|1|1/2|It cannot happen.","t|Probability can be more than 1.|F|It lies from 0 to 1.","m|P(E) + P(not E) equals|1|0|2|Complementary events.","s|Probability of an even number on a die as a fraction?|1/2|3 out of 6.","m|Probability from experiments is|empirical|classical|sure|By trials.","t|The sample space is the set of all outcomes.|T|Yes."])
